1
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:18,550
 Sleep well tonight, my boy. I think you've overdone it a little bit today.

2
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:19,790
 Well, maybe, yes.

3
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:24,370
 We were in the wood, bird spotting for most of the afternoon, and it was great fun.

4
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,760
 Then Mr. Brown said we could climb his tree and pick some apples.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,200
 Oh, and this morning at school, teacher told us all about the sea and about the big fissures.

6
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:38,830
 You'll be fine tomorrow.

7
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:40,540
 Sleep tight.

8
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:44,680
 Dreams, Peter.

9
00:00:55,310 --> 00:01:11,350
 Messages, Speedy?

10
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:12,410
 Just saucy.

11
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:13,820
 Sleep.

12
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,830
 We can put our feet up with no outside signal.

13
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:21,770
 Well, that's all right for some of us, but the brain cells are always on duty.

14
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:22,860
 Is that right, Meister?

15
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:23,460
 Hmm.

16
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:25,580
 Oh, yes, that's perfectly correct.

17
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:29,440
 Even though you may be fast asleep, your memory is sifting through piles of information.

18
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,440
 To the right, this can fly.

19
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,690
 Rots have always got wings.

20
00:01:53,130 --> 00:01:54,290
 Apples should be round.

21
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:59,940
 And red, some apples are green.

22
00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:05,220
 Apples, that's in order.

23
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:06,440
 And what about red blood?

24
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:06,920
 Fine.

25
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,980
 Then we've got green apples and green blood.

26
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:09,780
 What?

27
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:10,140
 What?

28
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,190
 That's just not on.

29
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:16,900
 I wonder what blithering idiot could have dreamed up green blood of all things.

30
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:17,660
 Out!

31
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:21,840
 Apples may be green or red, but there's only one colour for blood.

32
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,940
 Come along with me and we'll find a place for you.

33
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:27,910
 Birds can fly, that's good. Birds can be blue, okay.

34
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,930
 Birds have nests, quite correct.

35
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:32,790
 Birds have pin feathers, yes. Birds have pen feathers.

36
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:36,410
 What? I've never heard of pen feathers.

37
00:02:36,670 --> 00:02:36,910
 Out!

38
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:39,720
 You've been misfiled, son. No need to get upset.

39
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,380
 Just come along with me now.

40
00:02:41,890 --> 00:02:44,490
 I'll find you a place down in the pen and ink section.

41
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:46,580
 You've got nothing to do with birds, really.

42
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:55,270
 Chief, it's a little ink card, okay?

43
00:02:55,550 --> 00:03:00,120
 Hey, don't forget, ink's black as well.

44
00:03:01,310 --> 00:03:05,830
 Look over here. What do you make of this one, Chief?

45
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:10,070
 Never seen one like that before. I haven't the faintest idea.

46
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,290
 Wait over there. It's chalk.

47
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,240
 Yes, chalk is white and chalk and white.

48
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:23,470
 This sifting and sorting is boring and takes forever. Why don't we have some fun in the meantime?

49
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:25,230
 Let's play a little game then, guys.

50
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:28,930
 Why don't we rather make up a dream? We've got everything we need right here.

51
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:31,550
 It's a dream.

52
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:36,010
 Well, we sort of mix up all kinds of memories. Higgledy-piggledy.

53
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:37,210
 It's a fantasy, you see.

54
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:39,910
 And we can do things that are impossible in real life.

55
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:41,730
 You've got no idea what fun it is.

56
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:42,690
 Let's see.

57
00:03:42,790 --> 00:03:45,830
 We've got an apple, a bird, a tree, and a whole wood.

58
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,560
 Is that enough to make a dream?

59
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:52,960
 It's plenty, but we can do a lot better by adding some old memories.

60
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,080
 What do you say, fellas?

61
00:04:17,220 --> 00:05:12,870
 Thus, can they come too?

62
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:14,590
 Of course.

63
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:25,860
 Mr. Bird, tell me what the next stop is, would you?

64
00:05:26,170 --> 00:05:28,190
 What? You'll be amazed, my son.

65
00:05:28,190 --> 00:07:41,230
 Lord and Master, I swear fealty to thee, most excellent Duke of Nervdom.

66
00:07:42,030 --> 00:07:46,170
 This crown and rule over thy faithful subjects as supreme monarch.

67
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:46,960
 Huh?

68
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:57,850
 Maestro, please wake up right away. The dream's over.

69
00:07:58,910 --> 00:08:00,740
 Maestro, look there!

70
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:15,870
 Except, what on earth's going on here? Why did you wake me up?

71
00:08:15,970 --> 00:08:18,560
 The dream is over, Maestro, but he's gone back to sleep.

72
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:24,120
 That's perfectly all right. In fact, there was no need to wake me up for that. It wasn't an emergency.

73
00:08:24,670 --> 00:08:30,350
 Now, the brain can get back to the task of sorting out all the information, and we might even dream again.

74
00:08:30,470 --> 00:08:33,530
 Talking about young Peter, of course, not myself.

75
00:08:33,830 --> 00:08:35,710
 He has got the advantage of youth.

76
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:38,210
 It takes him no time at all to drop off to sleep,

77
00:08:38,310 --> 00:08:40,230
 whereas with an old fellow like me,

78
00:08:40,810 --> 00:08:44,780
 once he's been woken up in the weird mode...

79
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:52,470
 Cloud here, but dear me, I've forgotten where we're supposed to put them.

80
00:08:52,650 --> 00:08:54,040
 Sorry, I'm stuck too.

81
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:55,760
 Where on earth can I put this lightning bolt?

82
00:08:56,020 --> 00:08:58,670
 You could file it under heat, light, or electricity.

83
00:08:59,260 --> 00:09:01,450
 This thing flies, but it's no bird.

84
00:09:01,730 --> 00:09:03,390
 More like a mouse with wings.

85
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:05,600
 Look out here, beautiful eyes, old owl!

86
00:09:08,570 --> 00:09:11,740
 And the big bad wolf.

87
00:10:03,990 --> 00:10:18,850
 Darling, it was only a bad dream.

88
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:23,260
 A lot of mixed up memories, that's...

89
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:28,660
 Why does it made something like that be only a dream?

90
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,940
 Your body goes on working while you're asleep.

91
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:33,320
 Heart keeps on pumping blood round the body.

92
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:35,420
 And your brain doesn't shut down for the night.

93
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:38,120
 It's busy by sorting out all kinds of impressions.

94
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:40,480
 And sometimes it gets them muddled up.

95
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:41,830
 It can cause a nightmare.

96
00:10:42,180 --> 00:10:44,340
 It's a good job we don't dream every night, Mum.

97
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:47,070
 Oh, but we do dream every night, my darling.

98
00:10:47,330 --> 00:10:49,450
 It's just that we forget our dreams, that's all.

99
00:10:49,660 --> 00:10:53,820
 Nobody knows exactly how dreams work, Peter, but they help to keep us healthy.

100
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:59,490
 More nightmares for you, only sweet dreams from now on.

101
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:20,540
 Blood vessels bring oxygen to Peter's brain cells,

102
00:11:20,890 --> 00:11:26,240
 which burn this gas for energy and send back carbon dioxide into the bloodstream.

103
00:11:37,730 --> 00:11:45,050
 Dear young fellow, perhaps I should point out to you at the risk of being found oppressively informative

104
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:51,450
 that the bubble which you so impetuously kicked is not light oxygen, as you assumed, but heavy carbon dioxide.

105
00:11:54,090 --> 00:12:01,080
 It might not be a bad idea to take it along to the lungs and exchange it for a bubble of oxygen.

106
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:06,650
 Sir Globus, I remember what you told us about the body when it sleeps.

107
00:12:06,830 --> 00:12:09,150
 It goes on using oxygen even though it's unconscious.

108
00:12:09,370 --> 00:12:11,270
 So you see, I do listen to what you say.

109
00:12:25,310 --> 00:12:33,110
 The last one I need for a full load of oxygen.

110
00:12:35,550 --> 00:12:36,890
 Mitochondria needs some energy.

111
00:12:48,930 --> 00:12:50,710
 Mitochondrium plant number 227.

112
00:12:51,390 --> 00:12:52,880
 Are you listening or not?

113
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:58,240
 Mitochondrium plant number 227.

114
00:12:58,480 --> 00:12:59,220
 That's quite right.

115
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:01,640
 Sorry I can't stick around to play yo-yos,

116
00:13:01,930 --> 00:13:04,490
 but this oxygen is needed urgently and I'm in a hurry.

117
00:13:04,830 --> 00:13:05,290
 Bye.

118
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:10,480
 Not before time.

119
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,840
 Some of us take our jobs seriously, you know.

120
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:16,890
 Nobody cares about us night shift workers.

121
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:20,210
 They don't even know we exist, and I'm still waiting for my sugar.

122
00:13:21,110 --> 00:13:23,890
 I'm expected to produce miracles, keep this furnace going.

123
00:13:23,990 --> 00:13:25,470
 What am I to burn in it, horse feathers?

124
00:13:25,690 --> 00:13:27,780
 Ah, am I by any chance invisible?

125
00:13:46,670 --> 00:13:59,440
 Come on, maestro.

126
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:01,700
 Oh, it's just routine repairs and maintenance.

127
00:14:02,170 --> 00:14:04,450
 Even while the body's asleep, are the parts wearing out?

128
00:14:04,730 --> 00:14:06,390
 Parts are always wearing out.

129
00:14:06,570 --> 00:14:08,810
 The whole body needs to be repaired constantly,

130
00:14:09,050 --> 00:14:12,130
 and the best time to carry out repairs is when the body isn't so busy.

131
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:12,800
 When it's asleep.

132
00:14:13,470 --> 00:14:14,050
 Stop!

133
00:14:14,050 --> 00:14:17,160
 Can't accept any deliveries. The furnace is out of order.

134
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:20,720
 Well, I'm sorry, but I've got my orders. What am I supposed to do now? Wait.

135
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:25,440
 You'd better take your loads, the pair of you, and go and drop them off at some other furnace that's in working order.

136
00:14:25,690 --> 00:14:28,340
 What do you expect me to do? Just look at it.

137
00:14:34,970 --> 00:14:40,080
 This store's had it. We'll have to fit a new one, I'm afraid.

138
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:50,810
 Here, Chief. There's a big crack in the wall. It's bad.

139
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:53,860
 We need lots of protein to fix that. You'd better order some.

140
00:15:32,990 --> 00:17:03,900
 Doesn't work anymore.

141
00:17:04,270 --> 00:17:07,720
 We'll fix it or get a new one, Peter. Nothing lasts forever.

142
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:13,330
 This water pipe's completely bunged up.

143
00:17:13,350 --> 00:17:16,120
 Everything gets bunged up if you give it enough time

144
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:20,540
 It's new

145
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:22,780
 Oh no, not quite as good as new

146
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:24,540
 Sir, I want you to remember that

147
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:28,420
 Even though you repair things, they're bound to wear out completely in the end

148
00:17:28,420 --> 00:17:40,460
 That, the ribosomes have all become discombobulated

149
00:17:40,460 --> 00:18:14,570
 In assembly lines of this cell, protein production has broken down completely

150
00:18:14,570 --> 00:18:16,930
 This is a disaster, we'll just have to wake him

151
00:18:16,930 --> 00:18:19,170
 Alright, alright, I'll do it

152
00:18:19,170 --> 00:18:21,450
 Maestro, maestro

153
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:24,180
 Problem on our hands, maestro

154
00:18:24,180 --> 00:18:26,690
 Ribosome assembly lines have broken down

155
00:18:26,690 --> 00:18:28,890
 And the RNA codes are getting scrambled.

156
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:31,150
 Tell us what to do, maestro.

157
00:18:31,390 --> 00:18:33,280
 The human body is like a house.

158
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:36,580
 It has to be built first of all, then it has to be maintained.

159
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:39,960
 Because parts wear out and they have to be replaced.

160
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:41,280
 What's next, then?

161
00:18:41,630 --> 00:18:43,180
 I'll tell you what's next.

162
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:44,940
 A few repairs and renovations.

163
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:49,260
 The body you'll find has an amazing capacity for renewing and healing itself.

164
00:18:49,450 --> 00:18:50,950
 So let's get to work, shall we?

165
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:55,370
 The RNA instructions for assembling proteins are all scrambled.

166
00:18:55,370 --> 00:18:59,530
 I want you to get some repair enzymes down there right away and clear up the mess.

167
00:18:59,530 --> 00:19:01,030
 That's all for the moment. Get going.

168
00:19:01,030 --> 00:19:05,820
 I don't think we'll ever get this straightened out, Mr. Supervisor.

169
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:08,390
 Come on. I've got the plans. We can get to work now.

170
00:19:08,390 --> 00:19:16,560
 Separate C and G. Right. Good.

171
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:21,150
 Now we can put the C over here, okay, which gives us A-C-C-G-U.

172
00:19:21,150 --> 00:19:23,720
 It's fine. It's what it says here as well.

173
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:25,320
 Oi, we need some glue over here.

174
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:28,120
 I refuse to sit next to him. He's horrid. Bye.

175
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:32,500
 Put him in there. He's not a C.

176
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:33,000
 C.

177
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
 The C one ran away.

178
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,500
 It has to be a C, I think.

179
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:38,690
 If you change the message, it could lead to something serious!

180
00:19:38,690 --> 00:19:42,280
 I haven't got time to worry about that! Look at the work piling up!

181
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:56,450
 What's the meaning of this?

182
00:19:56,450 --> 00:19:57,820
 That it's not my fault!

183
00:19:57,820 --> 00:20:00,120
 How can you say that when you stuck them together?

184
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:01,620
 You were the one who did the cutting!

185
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:03,450
 Please stop arguing and fix it!

186
00:20:03,450 --> 00:20:13,400
 Know what you've just done! You've created wild genes, you have!

187
00:20:13,400 --> 00:20:15,900
 Wild genes, did you say? What do they do?

188
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:23,240
 Wild genes? Oh, this could be very serious!

189
00:20:23,240 --> 00:20:25,290
 Yes, I'll mix up the message!

190
00:20:25,290 --> 00:20:27,790
 Wild genes? This could be disastrous!

191
00:20:27,790 --> 00:20:30,080
 That's just what I was afraid of, Maestro!

192
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:32,080
 You took the word straight out of my mouth.

193
00:20:32,080 --> 00:20:33,360
 Judge! Too late already.

194
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:35,670
 We must hope that it won't be too bad.

195
00:20:35,670 --> 00:20:37,670
 It was to be expected, actually.

196
00:20:37,670 --> 00:20:39,670
 No matter how many times you repair something,

197
00:20:39,670 --> 00:20:43,340
 it's going to go seriously wrong at some stage or a whole thumbs.

198
00:20:47,650 --> 00:20:50,990
 Well, here we are, me hardies, and we're all set for some fun.

199
00:20:50,990 --> 00:20:53,650
 Follow me!

200
00:20:57,140 --> 00:21:01,430
 We can deal with those pilot proteins by producing enzymes to control them,

201
00:21:01,430 --> 00:21:04,430
 but we'd better be quick about it before they affect the nervous system.

202
00:21:04,430 --> 00:22:02,230
 If it's possible, then there's no harm!

203
00:22:02,230 --> 00:22:02,880
 then there's no harm.

204
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:08,820
 It's under control again.

205
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:10,720
 Do you know what simply astonishes me?

206
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:13,300
 The sheer lightning speed of the body's reaction.

207
00:22:13,320 --> 00:22:16,320
 No sooner had the body made a mistake by producing harmful proteins

208
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:20,160
 than it corrected its own error with an enzyme which simply wiped them out.

209
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:25,260
 It's fantastic, isn't it, when you consider how swiftly the organism repairs itself.

210
00:22:25,260 --> 00:22:28,560
 How many machines, I ask you, are capable of repairing themselves?

211
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:31,940
 The human body is more wonderful than any of man's inventions.

212
00:22:32,280 --> 00:22:33,860
 Now let's run down our checklist, shall we?

213
00:22:34,890 --> 00:22:36,090
 Mitochondria, okay.

214
00:22:36,730 --> 00:22:37,810
 Ribosomes, okay.

215
00:22:38,050 --> 00:22:44,940
 circulation okay heart okay all systems go everything's back to normal maestro that's

216
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:50,440
 exactly what i was expecting we've just seen how quickly a young organism can repair damage to

217
00:22:50,440 --> 00:22:56,710
 itself but things are different with older people why is that maestro because the cells get worn out

218
00:22:56,710 --> 00:23:02,110
 you simply can't go on repairing them indefinitely that's out of the question each cell wears out

219
00:23:02,110 --> 00:23:08,260
 and so does the whole body normal wear and tear takes its toll as well as abnormal damage in the

220
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:13,120
 case of those who abuse their bodies, then there's damage resulting from the body's own mistakes.

221
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:18,560
 Heparin, for example, has its uses, but it has to be eliminated when it tries to stop the healing

222
00:23:18,560 --> 00:23:28,900
 process. Then there's the never-ending battle against alien invaders, which is very tiring for

223
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:35,870
 you. And in the long run, it's the entire construction, the whole body, which becomes

224
00:23:35,870 --> 00:23:42,590
 more difficult to keep in good condition and takes longer to repair. Then one day, alas,

225
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:59,450
 it wears out completely. That's like you, Meister. With us.

226
00:23:59,450 --> 00:24:04,340
 Oh, no, Peter. I'm long past the age for playing football. It's a pity, but there it is.

227
00:24:04,340 --> 00:24:08,450
 Pa, why is there an age for playing football and an age for not playing it?

228
00:24:08,450 --> 00:24:14,850
 Peter, you've got all the time you need to find out the answer to that question.

